\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Let $D$ be a pseudo-effective relative real Cartier divisor on a normal variety $X/Z$. Must there exist a projective birational map $f:W\to X$ and a numerical decomposition $f^*D\equiv_Z P+N$ with $P$ nef over $Z$ and $N$ effective? Such a decomposition is called a weak Zariski decomposition.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{https://doi.org/10.4171/owr/2009/44}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Let $D$ be a pseudo-effective relative real Cartier divisor on a normal variety $X/Z$. Must there exist a projective birational map $f:W\to X$ and a numerical decomposition $f^*D\equiv_Z P+N$ with $P$ nef over $Z$ and $N$ effective? Such a decomposition is called a weak Zariski decomposition.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 30001351 (OWR-4127-002). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
